{! block("title") !}ooke — toke ecosystem{! end !}
{! block("content") !}

<div class="page-hero">
  <h1>ooke</h1>
  <p class="tagline">A CMS and web framework built on the toke language. File-system routing, flat-file and SQLite content stores, template engine with Markdown, island architecture. Zero runtime dependencies. Ships as a single native binary.</p>
  <div class="hero-actions">
    <a href="https://github.com/karwalski/ooke" class="btn btn-primary">GitHub</a>
    <a href="/ecosystem" class="btn btn-secondary">← Ecosystem</a>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="stat-row">
  <div class="stat"><div class="num">0</div><div class="lbl">Runtime deps</div></div>
  <div class="stat"><div class="num">1</div><div class="lbl">Binary</div></div>
  <div class="stat"><div class="num">3</div><div class="lbl">Modes: build+serve+repair</div></div>
</div>

<div class="container">
  <p class="caveat"><strong>The latency stat that used to sit in that row is withdrawn.</strong>
  It read &ldquo;&lt;5ms TTFB&rdquo; with no method, machine or sample size beside it. A benchmark
  has since been run (2026-09-19) and the number is not the problem &mdash; server-side time to
  first byte on loopback is 0.14&ndash;0.42&nbsp;ms median, worst p95 0.583&nbsp;ms &mdash; but the
  claim as published is: it was attributed to <code>ooke</code>, which could not serve this site at
  all, and a bare &ldquo;TTFB&rdquo; reads as what a visitor experiences, which loopback cannot
  measure. No replacement figure is quoted here until ooke itself can serve a real site and the
  number can carry its method. Harness and data:
  <code>toke-ooke/bench/serving/</code>; rows: <code>docs/metrics-baseline.md</code>.</p>

<h2>Quick start</h2>
<div class="code-block">
<span class="cm"># Scaffold a new project</span>
ooke new mysite
cd mysite

<span class="cm"># Generate content types, pages, APIs, and islands</span>
ooke gen type post title:str date:date tags:list
ooke gen page blog
ooke gen api posts
ooke gen island counter

<span class="cm"># Start the development server</span>
ooke serve

<span class="cm"># Build static HTML</span>
ooke build
</div>

<h2>What is ooke?</h2>
<p>ooke is the web framework layer of the toke ecosystem. Write route handlers in toke, templates in <code>.tkt</code> files, and content in Markdown or JSON with validated frontmatter. ooke compiles everything to a single native binary — no Node.js, no Python, no nginx required.</p>

<div class="features">
  <div class="feature">
    <h3>File-system routing</h3>
    <p><code>pages/blog/[slug].tk</code> becomes <code>/blog/:slug</code>. Convention over configuration. Index files map to directory roots. Dynamic segments use <code>[name]</code> notation.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="feature">
    <h3>Dynamic route handlers</h3>
    <p>Register routes programmatically with <code>http.get(path; handler)</code>, <code>http.post</code>, <code>http.put</code>, <code>http.delete</code>, and <code>http.patch</code>. Full HTTP method support with function pointer dispatch. Combine file-system and dynamic routes in the same project.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="feature">
    <h3>Template engine</h3>
    <p>Templates use <code>&#123;= expr =&#125;</code> for expressions, <code>&#123;! directive !&#125;</code> for layout/blocks/partials. Layout inheritance, named blocks, partials, and a Markdown filter built in.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="feature">
    <h3>Flat-file and SQLite content stores</h3>
    <p>Content lives as <code>.md</code> or <code>.json</code> files with validated frontmatter. Set <code>store.backend = "sqlite"</code> in <code>ooke.toml</code> for a SQLite-backed store with automatic schema migration. Same query API either way: <code>store.all("posts")</code>, <code>store.slug("posts"; "hello-world")</code>.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="feature">
    <h3>Content validation with models</h3>
    <p>Define content types in <code>models/*.toml</code> using TOML model definitions. Frontmatter is validated at build time — missing required fields, wrong types, and invalid values are caught before deploy.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="feature">
    <h3>Island architecture</h3>
    <p>Add interactive components without shipping a full JS framework. Use <code>&#123;! island("name"; hydrate="visible") !&#125;</code> in templates. Islands hydrate on demand — <code>visible</code>, <code>idle</code>, or <code>load</code> — with a minimal hydration loader. No build step for client code.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="feature">
    <h3>Build and serve modes</h3>
    <p><code>ooke build</code> renders static HTML with CSS inlining and minification. <code>ooke serve</code> runs a live HTTP server with an in-memory page cache. TLS supported. Worker count auto-detected from CPU cores (<code>workers=0</code> in <code>ooke.toml</code>).</p>
  </div>
  <div class="feature">
    <h3>Error and access logging</h3>
    <p>Separate <code>error.log</code> with automatic rotation. Access logs in NDJSON format for easy ingestion into log aggregators. Structured fields for method, path, status, and timing.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="feature">
    <h3>Custom error pages</h3>
    <p>Drop a <code>templates/errors/404.tkt</code> into your project for branded 404 pages. The same pattern works for other status codes.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="feature">
    <h3>Repair command</h3>
    <p><code>ooke repair &lt;file&gt;</code> runs an LLM-driven compile-fix loop: read the error, generate a patch, recompile, repeat until it builds. Useful for bulk-editing generated code.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="feature">
    <h3>Content type scaffolding</h3>
    <p><code>ooke gen type</code>, <code>ooke gen page</code>, <code>ooke gen api</code>, <code>ooke gen island</code> scaffold common patterns instantly. toke's 55-char syntax means an LLM generates a complete ooke site in fewer tokens with higher correctness.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="feature">
    <h3>Native binary output</h3>
    <p>ooke itself is a compiled C binary linked against the toke stdlib. No interpreter, no VM, no GC. Runs on macOS, Linux, and anything clang can target.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<h2>Template syntax</h2>
<div class="code-block">
<span class="cm">&#123;! layout("base") !&#125;</span>
<span class="cm">&#123;! block("content") !&#125;</span>

&lt;h1&gt;<span class="str">&#123;= page.title =&#125;</span>&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;div class="body"&gt;
  <span class="str">&#123;= body | md =&#125;</span>
&lt;/div&gt;

<span class="cm">&lt;!-- Island component with visibility-triggered hydration --&gt;</span>
<span class="cm">&#123;! island("counter"; hydrate="visible") !&#125;</span>

<span class="cm">&#123;! end !&#125;</span>
</div>

<h2>Route handler</h2>
<div class="code-block">
<span class="kw">m</span>=page.blog.slug;
<span class="kw">i</span>=http:std.http;
<span class="kw">i</span>=tpl:ooke.template;
<span class="kw">i</span>=store:ooke.store;

<span class="kw">f</span>=<span class="fn">get</span>(req:http.$req):http.$res{
  let post=store.<span class="fn">slug</span>(<span class="str">"posts"</span>;req.<span class="fn">param</span>(<span class="str">"slug"</span>));
  <span class="kw">&lt;</span>tpl.<span class="fn">renderfile</span>(<span class="str">"templates/blog/post.tkt"</span>;post);
};
</div>

<h2>Dynamic routes with full HTTP methods</h2>
<div class="code-block">
<span class="kw">i</span>=http:std.http;
<span class="kw">i</span>=store:ooke.store;

<span class="cm"># Register API endpoints with function pointers</span>
http.<span class="fn">get</span>(<span class="str">"/api/posts"</span>;<span class="fn">listposts</span>);
http.<span class="fn">post</span>(<span class="str">"/api/posts"</span>;<span class="fn">createpost</span>);
http.<span class="fn">put</span>(<span class="str">"/api/posts/:id"</span>;<span class="fn">updatepost</span>);
http.<span class="fn">delete</span>(<span class="str">"/api/posts/:id"</span>;<span class="fn">deletepost</span>);
</div>

<h2>SQLite store</h2>
<div class="code-block">
<span class="cm"># ooke.toml</span>
[store]
backend = <span class="str">"sqlite"</span>
path    = <span class="str">"data/content.db"</span>

<span class="cm"># Same query API — code doesn't change</span>
let posts = store.<span class="fn">all</span>(<span class="str">"posts"</span>);
let post  = store.<span class="fn">slug</span>(<span class="str">"posts"</span>;<span class="str">"hello-world"</span>);
</div>

<h2>Content model</h2>
<div class="code-block">
<span class="cm"># models/post.toml</span>
[fields]
title = { type = <span class="str">"string"</span>, required = <span class="kw">true</span> }
date  = { type = <span class="str">"date"</span>,   required = <span class="kw">true</span> }
tags  = { type = <span class="str">"list"</span> }
draft = { type = <span class="str">"bool"</span>,   default = <span class="kw">false</span> }
</div>

<div class="ecosystem-note" style="border-left-color:#f0c04a;">
  <p><strong>You are looking at it.</strong> This website &mdash; tokelang.dev &mdash; is built and served by toke on ooke. The page you are reading was rendered by the same framework described above: toke code compiled to a native binary, <code>.tkt</code> templates, Markdown content, file-system routing. No Node.js, no Python, no nginx.</p>
</div>

